Stop the frame-spill crop and the maximize handshake deadlocking each other.

wayland_surface_reconfigure() feeds the frame-spill crop's size to
wayland_surface_config_is_compatible(), which rejects a maximized surface even
one pixel short of the compositor's configure. The config is then never acked,
current.state never becomes MAXIMIZED, the crop's own current.state guard never
fires, and it crops again -- self-sustaining. Nothing ever commits and the
window stays a blank rectangle.

Reached whenever round(configure * scale) OVERSHOOTS the monitor, so it depends
only on the fractional scale. A build tester's After Effects at scale 2.25 got
round(1707*2.25)=3841 against a 3840-wide monitor, was cropped to 3836 -> 1705
against a 1707 configure, and hit this 3360+ times in one launch with zero
commits. The same app at 1.7 gets round(2259*1.7)=3840 exactly and never enters
the crop, which is why it reproduced on one machine only.

Skip the crop when it would put a maximized surface below its configure.

A SIZE test, not a state test. Premiere's overhang window -- the case the crop
exists for -- is also state=0x1 (measured 2026-08-12; an earlier state-based
guard suppressed exactly the crop it was meant to keep), but its crop leaves it
at or above its configure (3850->3840 = 2259 surface px against a 2256
configure), so it is unaffected.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c	2026-08-12 02:04:57.081077810 -0400
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c	2026-08-12 02:04:57.073727422 -0400
@@ -1045,6 +1045,13 @@
     int win_w, win_h, mon_w, mon_h;
 
     if (surface->role != WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_TOPLEVEL) return FALSE;
+    /* ⚠️ Do NOT widen this to surface->window.state, and do not assume the
+     * overhang case is un-maximized. Measured 2026-08-12: Premiere's overhang
+     * window -- the case this crop exists for -- reports state=0x1 (MAXIMIZED),
+     * so a state-based guard suppresses exactly the crop it is meant to keep
+     * (verified: WIN=3850x2116 cropped to 3840x2111 on 11.10-48, and gone when
+     * the guard was added). The real constraint is a SIZE one and lives at the
+     * call site in wayland_surface_reconfigure(). */
     if (surface->current.state & (WAYLAND_SURFACE_CONFIG_STATE_MAXIMIZED |
                                   WAYLAND_SURFACE_CONFIG_STATE_FULLSCREEN))
         return FALSE;
@@ -1070,6 +1077,60 @@
 }
 
 /**********************************************************************
+ *          neutron_crop_would_break_maximized
+ *
+ * TRUE if cropping the surface to `cw`x`ch` WINDOW px would make it smaller
+ * than a maximized config the compositor is waiting on.
+ *
+ * 🚨 Without this, the frame-spill crop and the maximize handshake deadlock each
+ * other. wayland_surface_reconfigure() converts the cropped size and hands it to
+ * wayland_surface_config_is_compatible(), which rejects a maximized surface that
+ * is even one pixel short of the configure -- so the config is never acked,
+ * current.state never becomes MAXIMIZED, the crop's own current.state guard
+ * never fires, and it crops again. Self-sustaining: nothing ever commits and the
+ * window stays a blank rectangle.
+ *
+ * Reached whenever round(configure * scale) OVERSHOOTS the monitor, so it
+ * depends only on the fractional scale. A build tester's After Effects at scale
+ * 2.25 got round(1707*2.25)=3841 against a 3840-wide monitor, was cropped to
+ * 3836 -> 1705 against a 1707 configure, and hit this 3360+ times in one launch
+ * with ZERO commits. The same app at 1.7 gets round(2259*1.7)=3840 exactly and
+ * never enters the crop at all, which is why it reproduced on one machine only.
+ *
+ * A size test, not a state test: Premiere's overhang window -- the case the crop
+ * exists for -- is ALSO state=0x1 (measured), but its crop leaves it at or above
+ * its configure (3850->3840 = 2259 surface px against a 2256 configure), so it
+ * is unaffected. 2026-08-12.
+ */
+static BOOL neutron_crop_would_break_maximized(struct wayland_surface *surface,
+                                               int cw, int ch)
+{
+    struct wayland_surface_config *configs[3];
+    int width, height, i;
+
+    wayland_surface_coords_from_window(surface, cw, ch, &width, &height);
+
+    /* The same three configs wayland_surface_reconfigure_xdg() tests. */
+    configs[0] = &surface->processing;
+    configs[1] = &surface->requested;
+    configs[2] = &surface->current;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)
+    {
+        if (!configs[i]->serial) continue;
+        if (!(configs[i]->state & WAYLAND_SURFACE_CONFIG_STATE_MAXIMIZED)) continue;
+        if (width < configs[i]->width || height < configs[i]->height)
+        {
+            TRACE("hwnd=%p crop %dx%d -> %dx%d would be short of maximized config %dx%d;"
+                  " keeping the full window size\n", surface->hwnd, cw, ch, width, height,
+                  configs[i]->width, configs[i]->height);
+            return TRUE;
+        }
+    }
+    return FALSE;
+}
+
+/**********************************************************************
  *          wayland_surface_reconfigure_geometry
  *
  * Sets the xdg_surface geometry
@@ -1369,7 +1430,10 @@
      * itself lives in wayland_surface_attach_shm). Shrink the viewport destination
      * and xdg geometry to that subregion's logical size so the visible content maps
      * 1:1 onto the monitor instead of overhanging its right/bottom edge. */
-    if (wayland_surface_get_frame_spill_rect(surface, &spill))
+    if (wayland_surface_get_frame_spill_rect(surface, &spill) &&
+        !neutron_crop_would_break_maximized(surface,
+                                            spill.right - spill.left,
+                                            spill.bottom - spill.top))
     {
         win_width = spill.right - spill.left;
         win_height = spill.bottom - spill.top;
